Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> I don't think that a textual data representation would solve the issue.
> You'd need some synchronisation points or, as you suggested below with
> s-expressions, other meta-structure to obtain the necessary redundancy
> to recover from partial losses. For a software process to restore data
> it's not relevant whether the contained data is textual or binary. The
> textual representation would support manual corrections by humans; but
> given how exceptionally rare corruptions occur I think there should be
> some additional real gain to implement such a new structure.

I was thinking more in terms of aiding manual recovery when I agreed
that a textual representation was the Right Thing.  For an automated
recovery system, the advantage over some binary representation isn't
quite as certain.

> Portability of save files would be such a gain. But you cannot compare
> games played on different systems because of compile time switches that
> affect what is available. So we'd either need some canonical version to
> play, or, accept that some components of a game stored on one machine
> will be missing on another (and hoping that the game design won't show
> any inconsistencies between versions with different features).

Looking through the compilation options in config.h, only COMPRESS
would be a big problem as far as cross-platform saves go.  But the
solution to that is to just uncompress them before taking them to
another platform.

Other options like BITFIELDS and MACRO_CPATH affect how data is
stored internally, but if the savefiles stored data as anything other
than a raw dump of memory, that wouldn't be a problem.

The various gameplay options would be fairly simple to handle if a
save is loaded on a version that doesn't have them compiled in. It
would probably be a good idea to print a warning message if a save
is loaded on a version with different options, of course.

Here's a quick overview of the gameplay options and possible
resolutions:

SINKS and KOPS could be handled by just not loading the object or
   monster if it doesn't exist.
WALLIFIED_MAZE and REINCARNATION affect level generation, so the
   level can be left as it was when it was generated, even if the
   current game would have done it differently. The rogue level
   normally makes diagonal movement different, but a version without
   it compiled in could still load the map while leaving movement
   alone.
SEDUCE: Foocubi are loaded normally; this only affects the attack.
STEED: Only an issue if the player was riding it when the game was
   saved. Possible solution: put the steed in the nearest safe square
   to the player.
TOURIST: If it's a savefile, it just doesn't work. If it's a bones
   file, remove the corpse to prevent Turn Undead from causing
   trouble, but leave the items.
ELBERETH: Still engraved; it just doesn't do anything.

I haven't put a great deal of thought into those, so there may be
deeper problems that I've overlooked.


